This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:
   - Build an event promotion checkout feature (Wallapop-style): after a user publishes an event, show an upsell modal on the event's own detail page (not blocking the publish form) inviting them to promote/boost their event; clicking through leads to a dedicated `/e/[eventId]/promote` page with benefits, a static MVP price, and a "Confirm and Pay" button that hands off to a Spring Boot backend endpoint (`POST /events/{eventId}/promotions/checkout`) which owns all Stripe/payment logic, returning a checkout URL to redirect to.
   - User explicitly instructed: work directly on the `develop` branch with no per-task review pauses ("wok in the directly to develop, but do it directly the whole changes with no reviews, we will review in the end everything").
   - After implementation, user caught two real regressions from a screenshot: (1) modal was appearing before navigation to the event page, not after landing on it as originally intended; (2) UI needed visual polish (icons, better design). User also asked to verify no local WIP was bundled into feature commits, and to apply DRY to duplicated E2E test code.
   - User then explicitly required: **"we need to use a PR and then merge that PR into develop as we allways do"** — i.e., revert local develop to match origin, keep all work isolated on a feature branch + PR, never push directly to develop.
   - User then said: **"then commit, push and create a new PR"** — resulting in PR #447 being created (`feat/event-promotion-checkout` → `develop`).
   - Most recently: **"run ai skill review loop in this PR"** — instructing use of the `pr-review-loop` skill against PR #447, to fetch AI bot review comments (CodeRabbit, Cubic, Greptile, Copilot), evaluate each against project conventions, apply agreed fixes, decline others with rationale, and resolve all threads.
   - Mid-session correction from user: **"dont close my chorme"** — a directive to never touch/control the user's actual running Chrome browser; I must not attempt browser automation tools that could affect their real browser session.

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - Next.js App Router with Server Components, Server Actions (`"use server"`), and Client Components (`"use client"`)
   - Discriminated union result types for Server Actions (no throwing to client) — established pattern via `EditEventResult`, `CreateEventActionResult`, now `PromotionCheckoutResult`
   - Ownership-gating pattern: resolve event by slug, verify caller-provided `eventId` matches `event.id`, verify `currentUser.id === event.owner.id` — copied from `editEvent` in `app/[locale]/e/[eventId]/edita/actions.ts`
   - `types/` directory governance: ALL types/interfaces must live in `/types`, ESLint-enforced (`no-restricted-syntax` for `TSTypeAliasDeclaration`/`TSInterfaceDeclaration` outside `types/`)
   - `lib/i18n/client-namespaces.ts`: whitelist of translation namespaces safe for client components; missing entries throw `MISSING_MESSAGE` at runtime (only caught by `test/i18n-client-namespaces.test.ts`, not typecheck)
   - One-time query-marker convention for triggering post-action UI (`newEvent`, `edit_suggested`, and now `promote=1`) in `EventClient.tsx`, with `router.replace` to strip the marker on dismiss so refresh/back doesn't retrigger
   - `next/dynamic` with `ssr: false` for lazy-loading modals only needed after specific user actions (matches existing `PreviewContent` pattern in `publica/page.tsx`)
   - Config-driven, list-returning pricing function (`getEventPromotionOptions()`) instead of a bare constant, specifically to be forward-compatible with future backend-defined tiers without changing calling code's shape
   - DESIGN.md design tokens: `containers.detail` = 520px, semantic color classes (`bg-primary/10 text-primary`, `text-foreground/80`, etc.), no `gray-*` utilities
   - Git history hygiene: splitting bundled/mixed commits into logically separate, honestly-labeled commits when working directly on a shared branch without a worktree
   - AI PR review loop (`pr-review-loop` skill): fetch unresolved GraphQL review threads, evaluate against project conventions (using project's own `code-review-evaluation` skill), fix/decline with rationale, resolve all threads, request re-review
   - ESLint rules specific to this repo: `react-hooks/set-state-in-effect` / `@eslint-react/set-state-in-effect` (currently blocking, needs an actual fix — NOT a suppression) vs. `@eslint-react/hooks-extra/no-direct-set-state-in-use-effect` (explicitly turned "off" in `eslint.config.mjs`, but that's a DIFFERENT rule than the one currently failing)
   - Repeated personal mistake pattern this session: using `// eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` when this project's actual dependency-array lint rule has a different name — the correct fix each time was to just include the real (stable) dependencies rather than disable the rule

